# Diagnosing connectivity failures — don't assume "server down"

When a configured `host:port` is unreachable but the host responds to ping,
**question the URL/port itself before concluding the service is down or
firewalled.** A wrong port in config is far more common than a dead server.

## What went wrong once (learn from it)

`IPAYMU_PROXY_URL` was set to `http://203.194.115.32:3000`. Port 3000 was
unreachable, so it was wrongly diagnosed as "VPS proxy down / firewall blocking
the IP." The real fix was removing `:3000` — the proxy is served on **port 80**
(`http://203.194.115.32`). It worked immediately, in prod and local.

## Checklist before blaming the server/firewall

- Test the **other common ports** (80, 443) when the configured port fails —
  not just the one in the config.
- Try the bare host (`http://HOST`) and `https://HOST` before assuming the
  endpoint is dead.
- Ping success + TCP-connect failure on one port = that port is closed/wrong,
  NOT proof the whole service is down.
- Treat the configured URL as a suspect, not ground truth.

## iPaymu proxy fact for this repo

The iPaymu VPS proxy is reached at `http://203.194.115.32` (port 80, no
suffix). Keep `IPAYMU_PROXY_URL` without `:3000`.
